(_s1.chapters = _s1.chapters || []).push(
  { n:1, summary:"Elkanah, an Ephrathite of Ramathaim-zophim in mount Ephraim, worshiped yearly at Shiloh with his two wives. Peninnah had children, but the LORD had shut up Hannah's womb, and Peninnah provoked her year by year until Hannah wept and would not eat. At the tabernacle, Hannah prayed in bitterness of soul and vowed that if the LORD would give her a son, she would give him back to the LORD all the days of his life. She prayed silently in her heart, and Eli the priest mistook her for a drunken woman. When she explained that she had poured out her soul before the LORD, Eli blessed her and asked God to grant her petition, and she went away eating, her face no longer sad. Samuel was born. After weaning him, Hannah brought him to Shiloh and lent him to the LORD for as long as he lived.",
  context:"Set at Shiloh, where the tabernacle stood under Eli the high priest; the book is anonymous, though tradition links it with Samuel and later prophets.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 1:11", text:"And she vowed a vow, and said, O LORD of hosts, if thou wilt indeed look on the affliction of thine handmaid, and remember me, and not forget thine handmaid, but wilt give unto thine handmaid a man child, then I will give him unto the LORD all the days of his life, and there shall no razor come upon his head." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 1:15", text:"And Hannah answered and said, No, my lord, I am a woman of a sorrowful spirit: I have drunk neither wine nor strong drink, but have poured out my soul before the LORD." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 1:27", text:"For this child I prayed; and the LORD hath given me my petition which I asked of him:" }
  ],
  takeaway:"God hears the sincere desires of a humble heart; what we receive in answer to prayer, we can give back to Him.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Where did Elkanah's family go up to worship and sacrifice each year?", choices:["Shiloh","Mizpeh","Gilgal","Beth-el"], a:0, why:"Elkanah went up yearly out of his city to worship and sacrifice unto the LORD of hosts in Shiloh." },
    { q:"Why did Eli scold Hannah when he saw her praying?", choices:["She had brought an unacceptable offering","Her lips moved but her voice was not heard, so he thought she was drunk","She refused to stand when he entered","She had criticized his sons"], a:1, why:"Hannah spoke in her heart, only her lips moving, so Eli supposed she had been drinking." },
    { q:"What principle does Hannah's vow about her son teach?", choices:["Prayers should always be spoken aloud to be heard","Only priests may dedicate children to God's service","What we ask of the Lord in sincerity, we can also offer back to Him in gratitude","Vows should be made only after blessings are received"], a:2, why:"Hannah asked for a son and pledged to give him to the LORD all the days of his life, then fulfilled her word." }
  ] },
  { n:2, summary:"Hannah's prayer turned to praise: her heart rejoiced in the LORD, her horn was exalted, and she confessed there is none holy as the LORD, no rock like our God, for by him actions are weighed; He brings down to the grave and brings up, makes poor and makes rich, and will give strength unto his king. Samuel ministered before Eli, but Eli's sons, Hophni and Phinehas, were sons of Belial who knew not the LORD. Their servant seized raw flesh from worshipers by force, and men abhorred the offering of the LORD; the sons also lay with women at the tabernacle door. Eli rebuked but could not restrain them. A man of God announced judgment: both sons would die in one day, no old man would remain in Eli's house, and God would raise up a faithful priest instead. Hannah bore three more sons and two daughters, and Samuel grew in favor with the LORD and men.",
  context:"Continues at Shiloh, where the young Samuel serves under Eli the high priest while Eli's corrupt sons preside over the sacrifices.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 2:2", text:"There is none holy as the LORD: for there is none beside thee: neither is there any rock like our God." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 2:30", text:"Wherefore the LORD God of Israel saith, I said indeed that thy house, and the house of thy father, should walk before me for ever: but now the LORD saith, Be it far from me; for them that honour me I will honour, and they that despise me shall be lightly esteemed." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 2:35", text:"And I will raise me up a faithful priest, that shall do according to that which is in mine heart and in my mind: and I will build him a sure house; and he shall walk before mine anointed for ever." }
  ],
  takeaway:"The Lord weighs actions and honors those who honor Him; privilege in His service never excuses abuse of it.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How many additional sons and daughters did Hannah bear after Samuel?", choices:["One son","Two sons and one daughter","Three sons and one daughter","Three sons and two daughters"], a:3, why:"The LORD visited Hannah, and she conceived and bore three sons and two daughters." },
    { q:"How did Hophni and Phinehas treat the offerings of the people?", choices:["They demanded raw flesh before the fat was burned and took whatever the fleshhook brought up","They refused to eat any of the sacrifices","They offered every sacrifice exactly as the law required","They shared the offerings equally with the worshipers"], a:0, why:"Their servant demanded raw flesh, and if anyone objected, he threatened to take it by force, so men abhorred the offering of the LORD." },
    { q:"What lesson does the judgment against Eli's house teach about honoring God?", choices:["God excuses family misconduct in religious leaders","They that honour God He will honour, and they that despise Him shall be lightly esteemed","Old age automatically protects a priest from rebuke","Warnings from God apply only to the person who receives them"], a:1, why:"The man of God declared that because Eli honored his sons above the LORD, his house would be cut off and his sons would die in one day." }
  ] },
  { n:3, summary:"The word of the LORD was precious in those days, with no open vision. The boy Samuel ministered to the LORD before Eli, sleeping near the ark of God in the temple at Shiloh. One night a voice called Samuel, and three times he ran to Eli saying, Here am I; for thou calledst me, and three times Eli sent him back. On the third call Eli perceived that the LORD had called the child and told Samuel to answer, Speak, LORD; for thy servant heareth. The LORD told Samuel He would perform judgment against Eli's house, because his sons made themselves vile and their father restrained them not. Samuel feared to tell Eli the vision, but at Eli's insistence he told him every whit. Eli accepted the word, and Israel from Dan to Beer-sheba soon knew that Samuel was established to be a prophet of the LORD.",
  context:"Israel's spiritual famine — the word of the LORD was precious and there was no open vision — precedes Samuel's rise as the LORD's prophet in Shiloh.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 3:10", text:"And the LORD came, and stood, and called as at other times, Samuel, Samuel. Then Samuel answered, Speak; for thy servant heareth." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 3:13", text:"For I have told him that I will judge his house for ever for the iniquity which he knoweth; because his sons made themselves vile, and he restrained them not." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 3:19", text:"And Samuel grew, and the LORD was with him, and did let none of his words fall to the ground." }
  ],
  takeaway:"When the Lord speaks, answer with an obedient heart — even when the message is hard to deliver.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How many times did the LORD call Samuel before Eli realized who was speaking?", choices:["Once","Twice","Three times","Four times"], a:2, why:"The LORD called Samuel three times before Eli perceived that the LORD had called the child." },
    { q:"What did Samuel fear about telling Eli the vision, and how did Eli respond?", choices:["He feared losing his position, and Eli removed him","He feared the dark, and Eli comforted him with a blessing","He feared Eli's sons, and Eli banished them","He feared showing Eli the vision, but Eli insisted and accepted the word, saying, It is the LORD"], a:3, why:"Samuel feared to show the vision, but Eli pressed him to hide nothing, and when he heard it he answered, It is the LORD: let him do what seemeth him good." },
    { q:"What made Samuel's words reliable to all Israel from Dan to Beer-sheba?", choices:["The LORD was with him and let none of his words fall to the ground","He consulted a council of elders before speaking","He only spoke what the people already believed","He inherited the office from Eli directly"], a:0, why:"Samuel grew, the LORD was with him, and the LORD let none of his words fall to the ground, so all Israel knew he was established as a prophet." }
  ] },
  { n:4, summary:"Israel went out against the Philistines and pitched beside Eben-ezer, while the Philistines camped at Aphek. Defeated at a cost of four thousand men, the elders proposed fetching the ark of the covenant out of Shiloh so that it might save them. When Hophni and Phinehas arrived with the ark, all Israel shouted so that the earth rang, and the Philistines trembled, remembering the plagues of Egypt — but they steeled themselves and fought. Thirty thousand footmen of Israel fell, the ark of God was taken, and Eli's two sons were slain. A messenger with rent clothes and earth upon his head ran to Shiloh, where Eli, ninety-eight years old, sat watching and trembling for the ark. Hearing the report, Eli fell backward, broke his neck, and died, having judged Israel forty years. His daughter-in-law bore a son and named him I-chabod, saying the glory is departed from Israel.",
  context:"First war of the ark narrative: Israel battles the Philistines at Aphek near Eben-ezer while Eli and his priestly sons rule from Shiloh.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 4:3", text:"And when the people were come into the camp, the elders of Israel said, Wherefore hath the LORD smitten us to day before the Philistines? Let us fetch the ark of the covenant of the LORD out of Shiloh unto us" },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 4:11", text:"And the ark of God was taken; and the two sons of Eli, Hophni and Phinehas, were slain." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 4:21", text:"And she named the child I-chabod, saying, The glory is departed from Israel: because the ark of God was taken, and because of her father in law and her husband." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Sacred objects cannot substitute for a right relationship with God; ritual without obedience brings no victory.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"About how many Israelite footmen fell in the second battle after the ark arrived?", choices:["Four thousand","Thirty thousand","Three hundred","Ten thousand"], a:1, why:"After the ark came into the camp, the Philistines fought and there fell of Israel thirty thousand footmen." },
    { q:"Why did the elders fetch the ark into the camp, and what happened instead?", choices:["To honor Eli, and the Philistines returned it at once","To frighten the Philistines, who immediately surrendered","They expected the ark to save them, but Israel was defeated, the ark captured, and Eli's sons killed","To obey Samuel's command, and the army rejoiced"], a:2, why:"The elders hoped the ark would save them from their enemies, but the very day brought slaughter, the capture of the ark, and the deaths of Hophni and Phinehas." },
    { q:"What does Israel's defeat with the ark present teach about trusting symbols?", choices:["God always guarantees victory to those who carry sacred objects","The ark failed because it was old and worn","Physical objects hold power independent of obedience","Trust placed in a symbol rather than in the Lord Himself leads to disappointment"], a:3, why:"Israel treated the ark as a charm, but the prophecy against Eli's house came true instead, showing that objects save no one apart from the Lord." }
  ] },
  { n:5, summary:"The Philistines carried the captured ark to Ashdod and set it beside Dagon, their idol. Next morning Dagon lay fallen on his face before the ark; set up again, he fell a second time, his head and both hands cut off upon the threshold. Then the hand of the LORD was heavy upon Ashdod, smiting the people with emerods. The men of Ashdod sent the ark away to Gath, where very great destruction followed, and the men of the city, both small and great, were smitten with emerods. When the ark reached Ekron, the Ekronites cried out that it had been brought to slay them. The lords of the Philistines gathered and ordered the ark sent back to its own place, for a deadly destruction ran through the city and the cry of it went up to heaven.",
  context:"The captured ark travels through Philistine cities — Ashdod, Gath, Ekron — wherever Dagon and his priests hold sway.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 5:4", text:"And when they arose early on the morrow morning, behold, Dagon was fallen upon his face to the ground before the ark of the LORD; and the head of Dagon and both the palms of his hands were cut off upon the threshold; only the stump of Dagon was left to him." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 5:6", text:"But the hand of the LORD was heavy upon them of Ashdod, and he destroyed them, and smote them with emerods, even Ashdod and the coasts thereof." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 5:11", text:"So they sent and gathered together all the lords of the Philistines, and said, Send away the ark of the God of Israel, and let it go again to his own place" }
  ],
  takeaway:"There is no God beside the Lord; idols and their powers fall helpless before Him.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Where did the Philistines first set the ark of God?", choices:["In the house of Dagon at Ashdod","In the temple at Ekron","On the wall of Gath","In the field of Joshua"], a:0, why:"They brought the ark from Eben-ezer to Ashdod and set it beside Dagon in Dagon's house." },
    { q:"What happened to the idol Dagon after the ark was placed beside it?", choices:["Dagon glowed with new power before the ark","Dagon fell on his face before the ark, and later his head and hands were cut off","Dagon was carried away by the Philistine priests","Nothing happened until the priests removed the ark"], a:1, why:"Dagon fell face down before the ark twice; the second morning his head and both hands lay cut off on the threshold." },
    { q:"What did the ark's journey through Ashdod, Gath, and Ekron demonstrate?", choices:["The God of Israel was one deity among several","The Philistines eventually worshiped the ark","The hand of the LORD was heavier than any idol, and no city could contain His presence","Plagues struck only the priests, not the people"], a:2, why:"Every city that kept the ark was smitten with destruction and emerods until the lords begged to send it home." }
  ] },
  { n:6, summary:"After the ark had been seven months in Philistia, the priests and diviners said to send it back with a trespass offering: five golden emerods and five golden mice, according to the number of the lords of the Philistines. They also warned the men not to harden their hearts as Pharaoh and Egypt had. A new cart was built and yoked to two milch kine that had never borne the yoke, their calves shut up at home; if the kine went up the way to Beth-shemesh, the Philistines would know the LORD, not chance, had smitten them. The kine took the straight way, lowing, and the lords followed to the border. The Beth-shemeshites, reaping wheat harvest, rejoiced, offered the kine, and the Levites set the ark on the great stone. But men of Beth-shemesh looked into the ark and many died, so the city sent for the men of Kirjath-jearim to fetch it up.",
  context:"After seven months of plagues, the Philistines test whether the ark returns to Beth-shemesh by God's hand or by chance.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 6:9", text:"And see, if it goeth up by the way of his own coast to Beth-shemesh, then he hath done us this great evil: but if not, then we shall know that it is not his hand that smote us; it was a chance that happened to us." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 6:12", text:"And the kine took the straight way to the way of Beth-shemesh, and went along the highway, lowing as they went, and turned not aside to the right hand or to the left; and the lords of the Philistines went after them unto the border of Beth-shemesh." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 6:20", text:"And the men of Beth-shemesh said, Who is able to stand before this holy LORD God? and to whom shall he go up from us?" }
  ],
  takeaway:"God is holy, and He must be approached on His terms, not ours; reverence protects us.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What trespass offering did the Philistines send back with the ark?", choices:["Silver and gold vessels","Five lambs and five bullocks","Ten talents of iron","Five golden emerods and five golden mice"], a:3, why:"Their priests instructed them to return a trespass offering of five golden emerods and five golden mice, one for each of the five lords." },
    { q:"What did the men of Beth-shemesh do that brought a great slaughter upon them?", choices:["They looked into the ark of the LORD","They refused to offer sacrifices","They hid the ark in the field","They sent it back toward Gath"], a:0, why:"The LORD smote the men of Beth-shemesh because they had looked into the ark of the LORD." },
    { q:"What did the cry, Who is able to stand before this holy LORD God? teach Israel?", choices:["God's holiness makes casual or irreverent approach dangerous","Beth-shemesh had been chosen for destruction","The ark should never leave Kirjath-jearim","Only Philistines needed to fear the ark"], a:1, why:"After judgment fell even on Israelites who treated the ark irreverently, the men of Beth-shemesh asked who could stand before so holy a God." }
  ] },
  { n:7, summary:"The men of Kirjath-jearim brought the ark into the house of Abinadab and sanctified his son Eleazar to keep it; it stayed there twenty years while all Israel lamented after the LORD. Samuel called them to put away the strange gods and Ashtaroth and serve the LORD only, promising deliverance from the Philistines. Israel gathered at Mizpeh, fasted, and confessed, We have sinned against the LORD. When the Philistine lords came up against the gathered people, the frightened Israelites begged Samuel not to cease crying to the LORD for them. Samuel offered a sucking lamb as a burnt offering and cried unto the LORD, who thundered with a great thunder, discomfiting the Philistines before Israel. Samuel set up a memorial stone called Eben-ezer — Hitherto hath the LORD helped us — and the Philistines came no more into Israel's coast while Samuel judged. Israel's captured cities were restored, and there was peace with the Amorites.",
  context:"Twenty years after the ark's return, Samuel leads national repentance at Mizpeh and judges Israel from his home in Ramah.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 7:3", text:"And Samuel spake unto all the house of Israel, saying, If ye do return unto the LORD with all your hearts, then put away the strange gods and Ashtaroth from among you, and prepare your hearts unto the LORD, and serve him only: and he will deliver you out of the hand of the Philistines." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 7:9", text:"And Samuel took a sucking lamb, and offered it for a burnt offering wholly unto the LORD: and Samuel cried unto the LORD for Israel; and the LORD heard him." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 7:12", text:"Then Samuel took a stone, and set it between Mizpeh and Shen, and called the name of it Eben-ezer, saying, Hitherto hath the LORD helped us." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Turning wholeheartedly to the Lord brings deliverance, and gratitude should mark every victory we remember.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Where did Samuel gather all Israel to pray and fast?", choices:["Gilgal","Ramah","Mizpeh","Shiloh"], a:2, why:"Samuel said, Gather all Israel to Mizpeh, and I will pray for you unto the LORD, and they fasted and confessed their sin there." },
    { q:"How did the LORD deliver Israel when the Philistines attacked at Mizpeh?", choices:["By sending a storm of hail on the Israelites","By making the Philistine armies fight each other in confusion overnight","By raising an army of angels to fight","By thundering with a great thunder so the Philistines were discomfited and smitten before Israel"], a:3, why:"As Samuel offered the burnt offering, the LORD thundered upon the Philistines, and Israel pursued them to Beth-car." },
    { q:"What did Samuel's stone, named Eben-ezer, commemorate?", choices:["That the LORD had helped Israel, marking the place of their deliverance","The spot where the ark was hidden","The boundary of the tribe of Benjamin","The first place Samuel judged Israel"], a:0, why:"Samuel set the stone between Mizpeh and Shen and called it Eben-ezer, saying, Hitherto hath the LORD helped us." }
  ] },
  { n:8, summary:"When Samuel grew old he made his sons judges over Israel, but Joel and Abiah turned aside after lucre, took bribes, and perverted judgment in Beer-sheba. The elders gathered at Ramah and asked Samuel to make them a king to judge them like all the nations. The request displeased Samuel, but the LORD told him to hearken to the people, for they had not rejected Samuel but the LORD himself, that He should not reign over them — the same forsaking they had shown since Egypt. Samuel was to protest solemnly and show the manner of the king: he would take their sons for his chariots, their daughters for cooks and bakers, their fields and vineyards, and the tenth of seed and sheep, until the people became his servants and cried out with no answer from the LORD. The people refused to obey: they wanted a king to go out before them and fight their battles, and the LORD told Samuel to make them one.",
  context:"In Samuel's old age, from Ramah — with his sons judging corruptly in Beer-sheba — Israel's elders demand a king like the nations.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 8:7", text:"And the LORD said unto Samuel, Hearken unto the voice of the people in all that they say unto thee: for they have not rejected thee, but they have rejected me, that I should not reign over them." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 8:18", text:"And ye shall cry out in that day because of your king which ye shall have chosen you; and the LORD will not hear you in that day." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 8:20", text:"That we also may be like all the nations; and that our king may judge us, and go out before us, and fight our battles." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Demanding our own way against the Lord's counsel brings burdens we were plainly warned about.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Who asked Samuel to make them a king?", choices:["The tribes of Gad and Reuben","The elders of Israel","Jonathan and Abner","The priests at Nob"], a:1, why:"All the elders of Israel gathered together and came to Samuel at Ramah demanding a king like all the nations." },
    { q:"What did the LORD say the people were really doing by asking for a king?", choices:["Improving their national defense","Honoring Samuel's lifetime of service","Rejecting the LORD, that He should not reign over them","Preparing for the coming Messiah"], a:2, why:"The LORD told Samuel that in asking for a king the people had not rejected Samuel but the LORD Himself." },
    { q:"According to Samuel's warning, what would the manner of the king be?", choices:["He would serve the people and refuse tribute","He would share power equally with the judges","He would be chosen from Eli's priestly line","He would take their sons, daughters, fields, and a tenth of their increase"], a:3, why:"Samuel listed what the king would take — sons, daughters, fields, vineyards, servants, flocks, and tenths — until the people became his servants." }
  ] },
  { n:9, summary:"Kish, a mighty man of power of Benjamin, had a son named Saul, a choice young man, goodly, and taller than anyone in Israel. When Kish's asses were lost, Saul set out with a servant to find them, searching mount Ephraim, the land of Shalisha, Shalim, and the land of the Benjamites without success. In the land of Zuph the servant suggested visiting the seer in a nearby city, offering a fourth part of a shekel of silver. Maidens drawing water confirmed the seer was about to go up to the high place for a sacrifice. The LORD had told Samuel the day before that He would send a man of Benjamin for Samuel to anoint captain over Israel, to save the people from the Philistines. Samuel hosted Saul at the feast, kept the shoulder portion for him, told him the asses were found, communed with him on the rooftop, and at the edge of the city bade him stand still to receive the word of God.",
  context:"A lost flock of asses leads Saul of Benjamin — the tribe's least family — to an unnamed city where Samuel is offering sacrifice.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 9:2", text:"And he had a son, whose name was Saul, a choice young man, and a goodly: and there was not among the children of Israel a goodlier person than he" },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 9:16", text:"To morrow about this time I will send thee a man out of the land of Benjamin, and thou shalt anoint him to be captain over my people Israel, that he may save my people out of the hand of the Philistines" },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 9:27", text:"And as they were going down to the end of the city, Samuel said to Saul, Bid the servant pass on before us, (and he passed on,) but stand thou still a while, that I may shew thee the word of God." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Ordinary errands can lead to extraordinary callings; God directs steps we think are routine.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What were Saul and his servant searching for when they met Samuel?", choices:["His father's lost asses","A missing brother","Water for the flocks","A lost sword"], a:0, why:"The asses of Kish, Saul's father, were lost, and Kish sent Saul with a servant to seek them." },
    { q:"What had the LORD told Samuel the day before Saul arrived?", choices:["That Saul would steal the sacrifice","That He would send a man of Benjamin for Samuel to anoint captain over Israel","That the Philistines would attack that night","That Saul's asses had wandered into the temple"], a:1, why:"The LORD had told Samuel in his ear that the man from Benjamin was coming and that Samuel should anoint him captain over Israel." },
    { q:"What does Saul's ordinary search for animals becoming an anointing day teach?", choices:["Only dramatic quests matter to God","Servants should make all spiritual decisions","God weaves His callings into the ordinary errands of daily life","Lost property is always a sign of coming greatness"], a:2, why:"A search for strayed asses ended at the feet of the prophet, because the LORD had arranged the meeting." }
  ] },
  { n:10, summary:"Samuel took a vial of oil, poured it on Saul's head, and kissed him, declaring the LORD had anointed him captain over his inheritance. He foretold three signs: two men by Rachel's sepulchre would report the asses found; three men going up to Beth-el would give him two loaves; and at the hill of God he would meet a company of prophets, and the Spirit of the LORD would come upon him, so that he prophesied with them and was turned into another man. Samuel told him to wait seven days at Gilgal. All the signs came to pass that day — God gave Saul another heart, and Is Saul also among the prophets became a proverb. At Mizpeh, Samuel presented the chosen Saul, fetched from hiding among the stuff; acclaimed as head and shoulders above the people, he received the shout, God save the king. Samuel wrote the manner of the kingdom in a book before the LORD. Saul went home to Gibeah; children of Belial despised him, but he held his peace.",
  context:"Samuel privately anoints Saul near Ramah, then presents him to assembled Israel at Mizpeh before Saul returns home to Gibeah.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 10:1", text:"Then Samuel took a vial of oil, and poured it upon his head, and kissed him, and said, Is it not because the LORD hath anointed thee to be captain over his inheritance?" },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 10:6", text:"And the Spirit of the LORD will come upon thee, and thou shalt prophesy with them, and shalt be turned into another man." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 10:24", text:"And Samuel said to all the people, See ye him whom the LORD hath chosen, that there is none like him among all the people? And all the people shouted, and said, God save the king." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God's call may begin privately, but He confirms what He appoints; courage grows when He changes hearts.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Where was Saul hiding when Israel gathered at Mizpeh to present the king?", choices:["In his tent","Behind Samuel's altar","In a well","Among the stuff"], a:3, why:"When the lot fell on Saul he could not be found, and the LORD answered that he had hid himself among the stuff." },
    { q:"Which of the following was one of the signs Samuel said would confirm the anointing?", choices:["He would meet a company of prophets and the Spirit of the LORD would come upon him","Saul would dream of Samuel that night","A crow would bring Saul bread each morning","Saul's spear would bloom like a branch"], a:0, why:"Samuel foretold that Saul would meet prophets at the hill of God, prophesy with them, and be turned into another man." },
    { q:"What does Saul's new heart and prophesying with the prophets show?", choices:["Emotion proves a divine calling","God can equip and change a person for the work He assigns","Prophesying made Saul king automatically","Spiritual gifts belong only to priests"], a:1, why:"God gave Saul another heart, and the Spirit of the LORD came upon him for the calling he had received." }
  ] },
  { n:11, summary:"Nahash the Ammonite besieged Jabesh-gilead and offered a covenant only on the condition of thrusting out all their right eyes. The elders asked seven days' respite to seek help from Israel, and the messengers reached Gibeah, where the people wept. Saul, coming from the field, heard the tidings, and the Spirit of God came upon him; his anger was kindled greatly. He hewed a yoke of oxen in pieces and sent them throughout Israel, warning that the oxen of anyone who failed to follow Saul and Samuel would be treated the same; the fear of the LORD fell on the people, and they came out — three hundred thousand of Israel and thirty thousand of Judah. In the morning watch Saul divided the army into three companies and slaughtered the Ammonites until the heat of the day. When the people demanded death for those who had doubted Saul's reign, Saul refused, saying the LORD had wrought salvation in Israel that day. Then all went to Gilgal and made Saul king before the LORD.",
  context:"Newly anointed Saul defends Jabesh-gilead across Jordan against Nahash the Ammonite and is publicly made king at Gilgal.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 11:6", text:"And the Spirit of God came upon Saul when he heard those tidings, and his anger was kindled greatly." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 11:13", text:"And Saul said, There shall not a man be put to death this day: for to day the LORD hath wrought salvation in Israel." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 11:15", text:"And all the people went to Gilgal; and there they made Saul king before the LORD in Gilgal; and there they sacrificed sacrifices of peace offerings before the LORD; and there Saul and all the men of Israel rejoiced greatly." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Deliverance belongs to the Lord, so victory should end in unity and mercy, not revenge.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What condition did Nahash set for making a covenant with Jabesh-gilead?", choices:["Yearly tribute of silver","Their daughters in marriage","Thrusting out all their right eyes","Forty days of labor in his camps"], a:2, why:"Nahash agreed to a covenant only on condition that he thrust out all their right eyes, as a reproach upon all Israel." },
    { q:"How did Saul rally Israel to defend Jabesh-gilead?", choices:["By writing letters to every city elder","By paying mercenaries from Judah","By promising each man a share of Ammonite spoil","By hewing a yoke of oxen in pieces and sending the pieces with a warning throughout Israel"], a:3, why:"Saul cut a yoke of oxen in pieces and sent them through all Israel, and the fear of the LORD made the people come out with one consent." },
    { q:"Why did Saul refuse to execute the men who had despised him after the victory?", choices:["Because to day the LORD hath wrought salvation in Israel — the day belonged to God, not to Saul's honor","Because Jabesh-gilead opposed the execution","Because Nahash demanded mercy as a condition","Because the law forbade punishing anyone during wartime"], a:0, why:"Saul credited the day's salvation to the LORD, so no man was put to death, and the kingdom was renewed at Gilgal." }
  ] },
  { n:12, summary:"Samuel, installing Saul as king, invited all Israel to witness against him: whose ox or ass had he taken, whom had he defrauded or oppressed, whose bribe had he received? The people affirmed his integrity. He rehearsed the LORD's righteous acts: the deliverance from Egypt through Moses and Aaron, and the pattern in which the people forgot God, were sold into the hand of Sisera, the Philistines, and the king of Moab, cried out, and were delivered by judges. When Nahash came against them, they had demanded a king instead of the LORD their God. Samuel set the terms: if they feared the LORD and obeyed, both they and the king would continue; if they rebelled, the LORD's hand would be against them. At Samuel's call, thunder and rain fell during wheat harvest; the people greatly feared and begged him to pray for them. Samuel vowed to keep praying for them and teaching the good and right way, warning that if they did wickedly they and their king would be consumed.",
  context:"At Gilgal, the aged Samuel delivers a farewell address, vindicating his stewardship and setting covenant terms for king and people.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 12:20", text:"And Samuel said unto the people, Fear not: ye have done all this wickedness: yet turn not aside from following the LORD, but serve the LORD with all your heart;" },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 12:23", text:"Moreover as for me, God forbid that I should sin against the LORD in ceasing to pray for you: but I will teach you the good and the right way:" },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 12:24", text:"Only fear the LORD, and serve him in truth with all your heart: for consider how great things he hath done for you." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Past failure is not final; keep praying, keep teaching, and serve the Lord with your whole heart.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What sign did Samuel call for to underline the people's wickedness in asking a king?", choices:["A locust swarm","Thunder and rain in wheat harvest","Three days of darkness","A plague on the flocks"], a:1, why:"Samuel said it was wheat harvest and called on the LORD, who sent thunder and rain that day." },
    { q:"How did the people respond after the thunder and rain?", choices:["They demanded Samuel's removal","They asked Samuel to leave Gilgal","They begged Samuel to pray for them, confessing they had added to all their sins this evil, to ask a king","They accused Samuel of calling the storm"], a:2, why:"The people greatly feared the LORD and Samuel and asked him to pray that they die not, admitting their sin in asking a king." },
    { q:"What did Samuel call sin against the LORD regarding the people?", choices:["Refusing to build him a monument","Rebelling against King Saul","Failing to bring offerings to Gilgal","Ceasing to pray for them — God forbid that I should sin against the LORD in ceasing to pray for you"], a:3, why:"Samuel declared that he would keep praying for the people and teaching them the good and right way." }
  ] },
  { n:13, summary:"Saul chose three thousand men: two thousand with him in Michmash and a thousand with Jonathan in Gibeah. Jonathan smote the Philistine garrison at Geba, and Saul blew the trumpet through the land. The Philistines responded in overwhelming force — thirty thousand chariots, six thousand horsemen, and people like the sand of the sea — and pitched at Michmash. Terrified Israelites hid in caves, thickets, rocks, and pits, and some fled across Jordan. Saul tarried seven days at Gilgal, the set time Samuel had appointed, but Samuel did not come and the people were scattering. Saul therefore offered the burnt offering himself. Samuel arrived and asked, What hast thou done? Saul excused himself — the people scattered, the Philistines gathered, he forced himself to sacrifice. Samuel pronounced: Thou hast done foolishly; the kingdom would have been established for ever, but now it would not continue, for the LORD had sought a man after his own heart. Israel was so disarmed that no smith remained in the land; only Saul and Jonathan had swords or spears.",
  context:"Saul's early reign: Israel's small band faces the Philistine host at Michmash while Saul waits at Gilgal for Samuel's appointed offering.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 13:9", text:"And Saul said, Bring hither a burnt offering to me, and peace offerings. And he offered the burnt offering." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 13:13", text:"And Samuel said to Saul, Thou hast done foolishly: thou hast not kept the commandment of the LORD thy God, which he commanded thee: for now would the LORD have established thy kingdom upon Israel for ever." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 13:14", text:"But now thy kingdom shall not continue: the LORD hath sought him a man after his own heart, and the LORD hath commanded him to be captain over his people, because thou hast not kept that which the LORD commanded thee." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Fear is never a license to disobey; trusting God's timing matters more than relieving pressure.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How many days did Saul wait at Gilgal before offering the burnt offering himself?", choices:["Three","Seven","Ten","Forty"], a:1, why:"Saul tarried seven days, according to the set time Samuel had appointed, but Samuel did not come." },
    { q:"What excuse did Saul give Samuel for offering the sacrifice?", choices:["The priests had refused to help him","The people were scattered, Samuel had not come in the days appointed, and the Philistines gathered","Jonathan had commanded him to do it","He believed he was already the rightful high priest"], a:1, why:"Saul pointed to the scattered people, the delayed prophet, and the gathered Philistines, saying he forced himself to offer." },
    { q:"What did Samuel mean by saying, Thou hast done foolishly?", choices:["Saul lacked military experience","Saul forgot the proper sacrifice order","He let fear push him to disobey a direct command, forfeiting an everlasting kingdom","Saul offered too many animals"], a:2, why:"Because Saul had not kept the commandment of the LORD, the kingdom that would have continued for ever was taken from his line." }
  ] },
  { n:14, summary:"Jonathan, without telling his father, took his armourbearer across to the Philistine garrison between the sharp rocks Bozez and Seneh, convinced there is no restraint to the LORD to save by many or by few. Their sign: if the garrison invited them to come up, the LORD had delivered the enemy. Climbing on hands and feet, they killed about twenty men; a great trembling and an earthquake shook the Philistine host, whose soldiers melted away and beat down one another. Hebrews serving the Philistines turned, hidden Israelites emerged, and the LORD saved Israel that day. Saul had adjured the people not to eat until evening; Jonathan, unaware, tasted honey and his eyes were enlightened, and he declared his father had troubled the land. Faint men later ate spoil with the blood, which Saul restrained by building his first altar. When God gave no answer to Saul's inquiry about a night pursuit, lots exposed Jonathan; Saul vowed his death, but the people rescued him, for he had wrought with God that day.",
  context:"The Michmash campaign continues: Saul's six hundred watch from Gibeah while Jonathan strikes the Philistine garrison across the pass.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 14:6", text:"And Jonathan said to the young man that bare his armour, Come, and let us go over unto the garrison of these uncircumcised: it may be that the LORD will work for us: for there is no restraint to the LORD to save by many or by few." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 14:23", text:"So the LORD saved Israel that day: and the battle passed over unto Beth-aven." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 14:45", text:"And the people said unto Saul, Shall Jonathan die, who hath wrought this great salvation in Israel? God forbid: as the LORD liveth, there shall not one hair of his head fall to the ground; for he hath wrought with God this day. So the people rescued Jonathan, that he died not." }
  ],
  takeaway:"The Lord can save by many or by few; faith acts on His power, not the visible odds.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How many men did Jonathan and his armourbearer kill in the first slaughter?", choices:["Ten","Fifteen","Thirty","About twenty"], a:3, why:"That first slaughter, made by Jonathan and his armourbearer, was about twenty men within half an acre of ground." },
    { q:"What was Saul's oath regarding food during the battle day?", choices:["Cursed be the man that eateth any food until evening","Every soldier must eat before noon","Only the officers might eat honey","No one could drink water until victory"], a:0, why:"Saul had adjured the people, cursing any man who ate food until evening, so the fainting people tasted nothing." },
    { q:"Why did the people refuse to let Jonathan die?", choices:["Because he was next in line for the throne","Because he had wrought this great salvation with God that day, and the people rescued him","Because Saul's oath was spoken in anger and void by law","Because the priests of Nob absolved him"], a:1, why:"The people protested that Jonathan had wrought with God that day and rescued him so that not one hair of his head fell." }
  ] },
  { n:15, summary:"Through Samuel the LORD commanded Saul to smite Amalek and utterly destroy man, woman, infant, and beast, because of what Amalek did to Israel coming up from Egypt. Saul warned the Kenites to depart, smote Amalek from Havilah to Shur, but spared Agag the king and the best of the sheep and oxen. The word of the LORD told Samuel that He repented making Saul king; Samuel grieved all night, then met Saul, who claimed he had performed the commandment — but the bleating sheep and lowing oxen said otherwise. Saul blamed the people, who spared the best to sacrifice unto the LORD. Samuel answered that to obey is better than sacrifice and rebellion is as witchcraft: because Saul rejected the word of the LORD, the LORD rejected him from being king. When Saul grasped Samuel's mantle as he turned away, it rent — a sign the kingdom would go to a neighbour better than he. Samuel hewed Agag in pieces and came no more to see Saul.",
  context:"Samuel relays the LORD's judgment on Amalek for attacking Israel in the Exodus, and Saul's response is tested at Gilgal.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 15:22", text:"And Samuel said, Hath the LORD as great delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices, as in obeying the voice of the LORD? Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice, and to hearken than the fat of rams." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 15:23", text:"For rebellion is as the sin of witchcraft, and stubbornness is as iniquity and idolatry. Because thou hast rejected the word of the LORD, he hath also rejected thee from being king." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 15:35", text:"And Samuel came no more to see Saul until the day of his death: nevertheless Samuel mourned for Saul: and the LORD repented that he had made Saul king over Israel." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Obedience pleases God more than religious performance; partial compliance is still disobedience.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What did Saul spare from the utter destruction of Amalek?", choices:["The women and children","The cattle only","Agag the king and the best of the sheep and oxen","Nothing at all"], a:2, why:"Saul and the people spared Agag the king and the best of the animals, destroying only what was vile and refuse." },
    { q:"What excuse did Saul offer for keeping the best of the spoil?", choices:["He wanted wealth for the treasury","The animals were too tired to travel","He feared the people and obeyed their voice, and they spared the best to sacrifice unto the LORD","Samuel had authorized him to keep them"], a:3, why:"Saul twice insisted the people had spared the best sheep and oxen to sacrifice to the LORD, and admitted he feared the people and obeyed their voice." },
    { q:"What did Samuel teach with, Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice?", choices:["Sacrifices matter more than intentions","God values wholehearted obedience above religious rituals performed in place of it","Only prophets may judge kings","Sacrifice was abolished under Samuel"], a:1, why:"Samuel asked whether the LORD delighted in offerings as much as obedience, then declared that rebellion is as witchcraft." }
  ] },
  { n:16, summary:"The LORD told Samuel to stop mourning over Saul, fill his horn with oil, and go to Jesse the Beth-lehemite, who had a chosen king among his sons. Fearing Saul would kill him, Samuel was told to bring a heifer and announce a sacrifice. At Bethlehem the elders trembled at his coming, but he sanctified Jesse and his sons and called them to the sacrifice. Eliab looked the part, but the LORD warned that man looketh on the outward appearance, while the LORD looketh on the heart. Seven sons passed before Samuel and none was chosen; the youngest, David, was still keeping the sheep. When he came in, the LORD said, Arise, anoint him, and the Spirit of the LORD came upon David from that day forward. The Spirit then departed from Saul, and an evil spirit from the LORD troubled him. Saul's servants sought a skilled harpist, and David was brought to Saul, becoming his armourbearer; whenever the evil spirit came upon the king, David played, and Saul was refreshed.",
  context:"The LORD sends Samuel from his home in Ramah to Bethlehem to anoint a replacement king from among the sons of Jesse.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 16:1", text:"And the LORD said unto Samuel, How long wilt thou mourn for Saul, seeing I have rejected him from reigning over Israel? fill thine horn with oil, and go, I will send thee to Jesse the Beth-lehemite: for I have provided me a king among his sons." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 16:7", text:"But the LORD said unto Samuel, Look not on his countenance, or on the height of his stature; because I have refused him: for the LORD seeth not as man seeth; for man looketh on the outward appearance, but the LORD looketh on the heart." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 16:13", text:"Then Samuel took the horn of oil, and anointed him in the midst of his brethren: and the Spirit of the LORD came upon David from that day forward. So Samuel rose up, and went to Ramah." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God looks on the heart, not the appearance; faithfulness in hidden duties prepares us for His assignments.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Whose sons did Samuel examine in Bethlehem to find the LORD's chosen king?", choices:["Eli's","Jesse's","Abinadab's","Kish's"], a:1, why:"The LORD sent Samuel to Jesse the Beth-lehemite, for the LORD had provided a king among his sons." },
    { q:"Why was David, the youngest son, not brought before Samuel at first?", choices:["He was traveling with Saul's army","He was ill and could not come","He was the youngest and was out keeping the sheep","Jesse feared Saul would kill him"], a:2, why:"Jesse said there remained yet the youngest, who kept the sheep, and Samuel sent for him before sitting down." },
    { q:"What does the LORD's word, man looketh on the outward appearance, but the LORD looketh on the heart, teach?", choices:["Physical stature predicts spiritual power","Appearance should be improved before serving God","God chooses randomly among siblings","God evaluates character and devotion that people cannot see"], a:3, why:"Eliab's impressive appearance did not matter; the LORD refused him and chose the least likely son instead." }
  ] },
  { n:17, summary:"Goliath of Gath, a giant champion six cubits and a span, defied Israel's armies in the valley of Elah for forty days, demanding a man to fight him; Saul and all Israel were dismayed and afraid. David, Jesse's youngest son, was shuttling between Saul's court and his father's sheep, and Jesse sent him to the camp with provisions for three soldier brothers. Hearing the defiance, David asked who this uncircumcised Philistine was to defy the armies of the living God; Eliab accused him of pride. David's words reached Saul, and he volunteered. Recalling the lion and the bear he had killed as a shepherd, David said the LORD that delivered him from them would deliver him now. Refusing Saul's armour because he had not proved it, he took five smooth stones and his sling. Goliath cursed him by his gods; David answered that the battle is the LORD's. His stone sank into the giant's forehead; David cut off Goliath's head with Goliath's own sword, and Israel chased the fleeing Philistines to Gath and Ekron.",
  context:"Israel and Philistia face off across the valley of Elah, between Shochoh and Azekah in Judah, in a war decided by single combat.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 17:37", text:"David said moreover, The LORD that delivered me out of the paw of the lion, and out of the paw of the bear, he will deliver me out of the hand of this Philistine. And Saul said unto David, Go, and the LORD be with thee." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 17:45", text:"Then said David to the Philistine, Thou comest to me with a sword, and with a spear, and with a shield: but I come to thee in the name of the LORD of hosts, the God of the armies of Israel, whom thou hast defied." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 17:47", text:"And all this assembly shall know that the LORD saveth not with sword and spear: for the battle is the LORD's, and he will give you into our hands." }
  ],
  takeaway:"The battle is the Lord's; when we act in His name, odds and armor matter less than faith.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How many smooth stones did David choose from the brook?", choices:["Five","Three","Seven","Twelve"], a:0, why:"David chose five smooth stones out of the brook and put them in his shepherd's bag before drawing near to Goliath." },
    { q:"Why did David refuse Saul's armour?", choices:["It was cursed by the Philistines","He had not proved it and could not fight in unfamiliar gear","Saul asked him to take it back","It was too heavy for Goliath to fear"], a:1, why:"David tried Saul's armour, then said, I cannot go with these; for I have not proved them, and put them off." },
    { q:"What did David's confidence rest on as he faced Goliath?", choices:["His sling's superior range","Saul's promise of reward","Previous deliverances by the LORD and the conviction that the battle is the LORD's","The size of the Israelite army behind him"], a:2, why:"David cited the lion and the bear and declared he came in the name of the LORD of hosts, for the battle is the LORD's." }
  ] },
  { n:18, summary:"David's victory bound Jonathan's soul to his; Jonathan loved him as his own soul, covenanted with him and gave him his robe, garments, sword, bow, and girdle. Saul kept David from returning home and set him over the men of war, and David behaved himself wisely. When the women of Israel's cities sang that Saul had slain his thousands but David his ten thousands, Saul was wroth, saw the kingdom in David's reach, and eyed him from that day forward. Under the evil spirit, Saul cast a javelin at the playing David twice, and David avoided out of his presence. Saul feared David because the LORD was with him, made him captain over a thousand, while all Israel and Judah loved him. Saul promised his elder daughter Merab, then gave her to Adriel instead. Learning that Michal loved David, Saul demanded a dowry of an hundred foreskins of the Philistines, hoping David would fall; David brought two hundred and became the king's son in law. Saul feared David yet more and became his enemy continually.",
  context:"David enters Saul's court for good after Goliath's death — loved by Jonathan and Michal, watched jealously by the king.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 18:1", text:"And it came to pass, when he had made an end of speaking unto Saul, that the soul of Jonathan was knit with the soul of David, and Jonathan loved him as his own soul." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 18:7", text:"And the women answered one another as they played, and said, Saul hath slain his thousands, and David his ten thousands." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 18:12", text:"And Saul was afraid of David, because the LORD was with him, and was departed from Saul." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Jealousy poisons from within; true friendship rejoices in another's success instead of resenting it.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What dowry did Saul demand for Michal?", choices:["A hundred talents of silver","The heads of the Philistine lords","A captured city","An hundred foreskins of the Philistines"], a:3, why:"Saul asked no dowry but an hundred foreskins of the Philistines, secretly hoping David would fall by their hand." },
    { q:"What song of the women turned Saul against David?", choices:["Saul hath slain his thousands, and David his ten thousands","David is the glory of Israel","Who is like unto Saul, the chosen of the LORD?","David the mighty, Saul the fearful"], a:0, why:"The singing women ascribed ten thousands to David and only thousands to Saul, and Saul was very wroth from that day." },
    { q:"Why did Saul fear David even while using him in the army?", choices:["Because the LORD was with David and had departed from Saul","Because David refused to fight the Philistines","Because David plotted with Samuel","Because the people demanded David as king at once"], a:0, why:"Saul saw that David behaved wisely and that the LORD was with him, so his fear grew into continual enmity." }
  ] },
  { n:19, summary:"Saul ordered his son Jonathan and his servants to kill David. Jonathan warned David to hide, then pleaded his case before Saul: David had not sinned against him, had put his life in his hand to slay Goliath, and had wrought a great salvation for Israel — why sin against innocent blood? Saul hearkened and sware David should not be slain, and David returned to court. War came again; David smote the Philistines with a great slaughter. But the evil spirit came upon Saul as David played, and Saul hurled his javelin; David escaped that night. Michal let David down through a window, staged an image in the bed with a pillow of goats' hair, and claimed her husband was sick. David fled to Samuel at Ramah, and the two dwelt in Naioth. Three waves of messengers sent to seize David each began prophesying under the Spirit of God, and when Saul himself came, the Spirit was upon him too; he prophesied and lay down all that day and night.",
  context:"Open conflict between Saul and David: javelins, midnight escapes, and a strange prophetic episode at Samuel's home in Ramah.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 19:5", text:"For he did put his life in his hand, and slew the Philistine, and the LORD wrought a great salvation for all Israel: thou sawest it, and didst rejoice: wherefore then wilt thou sin against innocent blood, to slay David without a cause?" },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 19:10", text:"And Saul sought to smite David even to the wall with the javelin; but he slipped away out of Saul's presence, and he smote the javelin into the wall: and David fled, and escaped that night." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 19:23", text:"And he went thither to Naioth in Ramah: and the Spirit of God was upon him also, and he went on, and prophesied, until he came to Naioth in Ramah." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God can restrain evil and change hearts through prayer, courage, and even unexpected intervention.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Whom did Saul order to kill David at the chapter's opening?", choices:["The priests of Nob","His servants and Jonathan his son","The garrison at Geba","Doeg the Edomite"], a:1, why:"Saul spake to Jonathan his son and to all his servants that they should kill David." },
    { q:"How did Michal help David escape Saul's messengers?", choices:["She bribed them with silver","She hid him in a well","She dressed him as a woman","She let him down through a window and staged an image in the bed"], a:3, why:"Michal let David down through a window by night and laid an image with a pillow of goats' hair in the bed, saying he was sick." },
    { q:"What happened to each group of messengers Saul sent to Naioth in Ramah?", choices:["They saw the prophets prophesying and the Spirit of God came on them, and they prophesied too","They were struck blind at the gate","They joined David's men at once","They returned with news of David's death"], a:0, why:"All three groups of messengers, and finally Saul himself, were overcome by the Spirit of God and prophesied." }
  ] },
  { n:20, summary:"David asked Jonathan what sin had made Saul seek his life; Jonathan thought his father would tell him everything, but David insisted there was but a step between him and death. At the new moon feast David would hide, and Jonathan would sound out his father: if Saul said, It is well, there was peace; if he was very wroth, evil was determined. Jonathan renewed the covenant, asking that the kindness of the LORD be shown to his house for ever, and they agreed on a signal of arrows shot by the stone Ezel — arrows beyond thee meant flee. When David's seat stayed empty two days and Jonathan gave the cover story of a family sacrifice in Bethlehem, Saul's anger flared: while the son of Jesse lived, Jonathan would never be established, and Saul cast a javelin at his son. Jonathan then shot the arrows, gave the sign, and the friends met, kissed, and wept. Jonathan sent David away in peace, swearing, The LORD be between me and thee, and between my seed and thy seed for ever.",
  context:"David and Jonathan secretly test Saul's intent during the new moon feast at Gibeah, using a planned sign in an open field.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 20:14", text:"And thou shalt not only while yet I live shew me the kindness of the LORD, that I die not:" },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 20:33", text:"And Saul cast a javelin at him to smite him: whereby Jonathan knew that it was determined of his father to slay David." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 20:42", text:"And Jonathan said to David, Go in peace, forasmuch as we have sworn both of us in the name of the LORD, saying, The LORD be between me and thee, and between my seed and thy seed for ever. And he arose and departed: and Jonathan went into the city." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Covenant loyalty keeps promises that outlast our own lives and shelter those who come after us.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What signal did Jonathan use to warn David?", choices:["A trumpet blast","Three arrows shot beyond the stone Ezel","A lamp in the window","A red cloth on the wall"], a:1, why:"Jonathan would shoot three arrows; if he cried that the arrows were beyond the lad, David knew he must flee." },
    { q:"What did Saul's rage at the feast reveal to Jonathan?", choices:["That Saul intended to forgive David","That Saul had forgotten the new moon","That it was determined of his father to slay David","That Saul planned to abdicate to David"], a:2, why:"When Saul hurled a javelin at Jonathan over David's empty seat, Jonathan knew his father was determined to kill David." },
    { q:"What did Jonathan's request, that David show the kindness of the LORD to his house for ever, express?", choices:["A demand for payment of debts","Fear that David would steal his property","A wish to travel with David permanently","A covenant loyalty extending to Jonathan's descendants"], a:3, why:"Jonathan bound David to spare his family when the LORD cut off David's enemies, making a covenant with the house of David." }
  ] },
  { n:21, summary:"David fled to Nob to Ahimelech the priest and said the king had sent him on a secret mission, asking for bread. There was no common bread, only hallowed shewbread freshly set out before the LORD, which the priest gave him after David said his young men had kept themselves from women. Unluckily for that household, Doeg the Edomite, the chiefest of Saul's herdmen, was detained before the LORD that day and saw everything. David also asked for a weapon and received the sword of Goliath, wrapped in a cloth behind the ephod. He then fled to Achish king of Gath, whose servants quoted the song of David's ten thousands. Sore afraid, David feigned madness — scrabbling on the doors of the gate and letting his spittle fall on his beard — until Achish dismissed him, asking why mad men should be brought into his house.",
  context:"Fleeing north, David stops at Nob, the priests' city with the tabernacle, then crosses into Philistine Gath under king Achish.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 21:6", text:"So the priest gave him hallowed bread: for there was no bread there but the shewbread, that was taken from before the LORD, to put hot bread in the day when it was taken away." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 21:9", text:"And the priest said, The sword of Goliath the Philistine, whom thou slewest in the valley of Elah, behold, it is here wrapped in a cloth behind the ephod: if thou wilt take that, take it: for there is no other save that here. And David said, There is none like that; give it me." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 21:12", text:"And David laid up these words in his heart, and was sore afraid of Achish the king of Gath." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Fear can tempt even good people to deceive; desperation calls for trust in God, not plots.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What did David receive from Ahimelech besides the hallowed bread?", choices:["The sword of Goliath","A coat of mail","A map of Gath","Three hundred shekels of silver"], a:0, why:"Ahimelech handed over Goliath's sword, kept wrapped in a cloth behind the ephod, and David took it." },
    { q:"Why was David sore afraid in Gath?", choices:["Achish threatened to execute him at once","Achish's servants identified him from the song of his ten thousands","He had left his men without food","Samuel had warned him against the city"], a:1, why:"The servants of Achish quoted the dance song, Saul hath slain his thousands, and David his ten thousands, and David became sore afraid." },
    { q:"How did David escape from Achish's court?", choices:["By fighting his way out with Goliath's sword","By showing the king his anointing oil","By feigning madness, scrabbling on the gate doors until Achish dismissed him","By calling down darkness on the city"], a:2, why:"David feigned madness, letting his spittle fall on his beard, until Achish asked why a mad man should be brought into his house." }
  ] },
  { n:22, summary:"David escaped to the cave Adullam, where his brethren and all his father's house joined him, along with every one in distress, in debt, or discontented — about four hundred men, over whom he became captain. He lodged his parents with the king of Moab, and the prophet Gad directed him into the forest of Hareth in Judah. Saul, brooding under a tree at Gibeah, accused his Benjamite servants of conspiring with the son of Jesse; Doeg the Edomite reported that he had seen Ahimelech at Nob inquire of the LORD for David, give him victuals, and hand over Goliath's sword. Summoned with the priests of Nob, Ahimelech protested that David was the king's son in law, honourable in his house, and that he knew nothing of any conspiracy. Saul condemned them to death; his footmen refused to slay the priests of the LORD, but Doeg fell upon them, killing eighty-five who wore the linen ephod, then destroying Nob with the sword. Only Abiathar, Ahimelech's son, escaped to David, who took responsibility and promised him safeguard.",
  context:"From the cave Adullam to the forests of Judah, David gathers a following while Saul's fury falls on the priests of Nob.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 22:2", text:"And every one that was in distress, and every one that was in debt, and every one that was discontented, gathered themselves unto him; and he became a captain over them" },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 22:14", text:"Then Ahimelech answered the king, and said, And who is so faithful among all thy servants as David, which is the king's son in law, and goeth at thy bidding, and is honourable in thine house?" },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 22:23", text:"Abide thou with me, fear not: for he that seeketh my life seeketh thy life: but with me thou shalt be in safeguard." }
  ],
  takeaway:"One dishonest act can cost innocent lives; godly leaders own the harm and shelter the wounded.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How many priests wearing linen ephods did Doeg slay at Nob?", choices:["Twenty","Forty","Seventy","Eighty-five"], a:3, why:"Doeg fell upon the priests and slew on that day fourscore and five persons that did wear a linen ephod." },
    { q:"Who gathered to David in the cave Adullam?", choices:["Everyone in distress, in debt, and discontented — about four hundred men","Saul's chief captains","The priests of Nob","The army of Judah"], a:0, why:"All who were distressed, indebted, or discontented gathered to David, and he became captain over about four hundred men." },
    { q:"How did David respond when Abiathar reported the massacre?", choices:["He blamed Abiathar for visiting Nob","He owned the deaths of Abiathar's father's house and promised him safeguard","He sent Abiathar back to Gibeah","He wrote a protest to Saul"], a:1, why:"David knew Doeg would tell Saul, took responsibility for the deaths, and told Abiathar to stay with him in safeguard." }
  ] },
  { n:23, summary:"David learned that the Philistines were robbing the threshingfloors of Keilah. Though his frightened men doubted the venture, David inquired of the LORD, who twice said, Go, and save Keilah — and David saved the city. Saul read the move as God's deliverance of David into his hands and called all the people to war. With Abiathar bringing the ephod, David asked whether the men of Keilah would betray him; the LORD said they would deliver him up, so David and his six hundred slipped away, and Saul turned back. In the wilderness of Ziph Saul hunted him daily, but God delivered him not into his hand. Jonathan came to the wood and strengthened David's hand in God, affirming that David would be king over Israel and that he himself would be next; the two renewed their covenant before the LORD. Ziphite informers offered David's location, and Saul nearly trapped him in the wilderness of Maon — until word of a Philistine invasion pulled the king away, and David moved into strongholds at En-gedi.",
  context:"David plays rescuer at Keilah, then runs from the wilderness of Ziph to Maon as Saul pursues, strengthened once by a secret visit from Jonathan.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 23:2", text:"Therefore David inquired of the LORD, saying, Shall I go and smite these Philistines? And the LORD said unto David, Go, and smite the Philistines, and save Keilah." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 23:16", text:"And Jonathan Saul's son arose, and went to David into the wood, and strengthened his hand in God." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 23:17", text:"And he said unto him, Fear not: for the hand of Saul my father shall not find thee; and thou shalt be king over Israel, and I shall be next unto thee; and that also Saul my father knoweth." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Ask God before every advance and every retreat; His answers guard both courage and caution.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What town did David deliver from the Philistines?", choices:["Keilah","Ziklag","Jabesh-gilead","Gath"], a:0, why:"After twice inquiring of the LORD, David went down, fought the Philistines, and saved the inhabitants of Keilah." },
    { q:"What did Jonathan tell David when he visited him in the wood?", choices:["That Saul had died in battle","That Michal had been remarried","That the Ziphites had changed sides","Fear not... thou shalt be king over Israel, and I shall be next unto thee"], a:3, why:"Jonathan strengthened David's hand in God, affirming that Saul would not find him and that David would reign." },
    { q:"Why did David leave Keilah after saving it?", choices:["The LORD told him the men of Keilah would deliver him up to Saul","His men refused to guard the walls","The Philistines returned with a larger force","Samuel summoned him to Ramah"], a:0, why:"When David asked whether the men of Keilah would surrender him, the LORD answered that they would deliver him up." }
  ] },
  { n:24, summary:"Returning from the Philistine war, Saul took three thousand chosen men to hunt David in the wilderness of En-gedi. He entered a cave to cover his feet, unknowingly coming within reach of David and his men hidden in the cave's sides. David's men read it as the LORD's promised day, but David only cut off the skirt of Saul's robe privily — and even that smote his heart. He restrained his men: The LORD forbid that I should do this thing unto my master, the LORD's anointed. Then David called after Saul, bowed himself, and showed the skirt, protesting that there was neither evil nor transgression in his hand and leaving judgment to the LORD: The LORD judge between me and thee; but mine hand shall not be upon thee. Saul wept, confessed David was more righteous — thou hast rewarded me good, whereas I have rewarded thee evil — and acknowledged that David would surely be king. He asked only an oath that David would not cut off his seed; David sware, and they parted.",
  context:"Saul's three thousand track David's six hundred into the wilderness of En-gedi, where a roadside cave becomes a test of conscience.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 24:6", text:"And he said unto his men, The LORD forbid that I should do this thing unto my master, the LORD's anointed, to stretch forth mine hand against him, seeing he is the anointed of the LORD." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 24:12", text:"The LORD judge between me and thee, and the LORD avenge me of thee: but mine hand shall not be upon thee." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 24:17", text:"And he said to David, Thou art more righteous than I: for thou hast rewarded me good, whereas I have rewarded thee evil." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Never seize by force what God has promised to give in His own way and time.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What did David cut off while Saul slept in the cave?", choices:["Saul's spear cord","The skirt of Saul's robe","Saul's sword belt","A lock of Saul's hair"], a:1, why:"David arose and cut off the skirt of Saul's robe privily, and afterward his heart smote him for it." },
    { q:"Why did David's heart smite him for even cutting the robe?", choices:["The robe was a gift from Samuel","He considered Saul the LORD's anointed, and stretching forth a hand against him was forbidden","The cloth was needed for the tabernacle","His men had dared him to do it"], a:1, why:"David told his men the LORD forbade stretching forth a hand against the LORD's anointed, and he stayed them from attacking Saul." },
    { q:"What did Saul admit after David spared him?", choices:["That David had stolen his kingdom","That Jonathan had conspired with David","That Samuel had anointed the wrong man","Thou art more righteous than I... I know well that thou shalt surely be king"], a:3, why:"Saul wept, confessed David had rewarded him good for evil, and asked an oath that David would not destroy his seed." }
  ] },
  { n:25, summary:"Samuel died and was buried at Ramah. Near Carmel, Nabal — churlish in his doings — was shearing three thousand sheep and a thousand goats; his wife Abigail was a woman of good understanding. David sent ten young men to ask a provision, noting his men had guarded Nabal's shepherds. Nabal scorned the request — Who is David? — and David armed four hundred men to destroy his household. A servant warned Abigail that evil was determined against the house, for Nabal was impossible to reason with. Abigail hastened with bread, wine, sheep, corn, raisins, and fig cakes, fell before David, took the iniquity on herself, and pleaded that he shed no blood causelessly, for the LORD would certainly make him a sure house. David blessed the LORD that had kept him back and accepted her gift. Nabal's heart died within him when he heard; ten days later the LORD smote him, and David sent for Abigail to be his wife. He also took Ahinoam of Jezreel, while Saul had given Michal to another man.",
  context:"In the wilderness of Paran and Carmel, a wealthy shepherd's insult nearly costs his household everything, and Samuel's death closes an era.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 25:3", text:"Now the name of the man was Nabal; and the name of his wife Abigail: and she was a woman of good understanding, and of a beautiful countenance: but the man was churlish and evil in his doings; and he was of the house of Caleb." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 25:28", text:"I pray thee, forgive the trespass of thine handmaid: for the LORD will certainly make my lord a sure house; because my lord fighteth the battles of the LORD, and evil hath not been found in thee all thy days." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 25:33", text:"And blessed be thy advice, and blessed be thou, which hast kept me this day from coming to shed blood, and from avenging myself with mine own hand." }
  ],
  takeaway:"A wise, humble voice at the right moment can turn away anger and spare great bloodshed.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What provision did Abigail hurry away to David?", choices:["Two hundred loaves, wine, sheep, parched corn, raisins, and fig cakes","Silver and gold from her treasury","Arms and armor for four hundred men","Water carts and tents"], a:0, why:"Abigail loaded asses with two hundred loaves, two bottles of wine, five sheep, parched corn, raisins, and cakes of figs." },
    { q:"How did Nabal die?", choices:["In battle with the Philistines","The LORD smote him about ten days after his heart died within him","By the hand of David's four hundred men","Of a fall from his rooftop during the feast"], a:1, why:"When told of the danger his folly had brought, Nabal's heart died within him, and about ten days later the LORD smote Nabal, that he died." },
    { q:"What made Abigail's intervention effective?", choices:["Her family's wealth and rank","Her threat to denounce David to Saul","Haste, humility, a wise plea, and reminding David of God's purposes so he would not avenge himself","David's fear of public opinion"], a:2, why:"Abigail hastened, bowed before David, took the blame, and reminded him the LORD would make him a sure house — so he need not shed blood causelessly." }
  ] },
  { n:26, summary:"When the Ziphites again reported David's hiding place in the hill of Hachilah, Saul came down with three thousand chosen men. David sent spies, then went by night with Abishai into the sleeping camp, where Saul lay in the trench with his spear stuck in the ground at his bolster. Abishai begged one thrust to end it, but David refused: who can stretch forth his hand against the LORD's anointed, and be guiltless? The LORD would smite him, or his day would come to die, or he would perish in battle. They carried off the spear and the cruse of water untouched, for a deep sleep from the LORD had fallen on the camp, and David called to Abner from a hill afar off, rebuking him for failing to guard the king. Recognizing David's voice, Saul confessed, I have sinned... I have played the fool, and have erred exceedingly, and blessed David: thou shalt both do great things, and also shalt still prevail. David went his way.",
  context:"The Ziphites betray David's camp a second time; by night the hunted man stands over the sleeping king in the wilderness of Ziph.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 26:9", text:"And David said to Abishai, Destroy him not: for who can stretch forth his hand against the LORD's anointed, and be guiltless?" },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 26:11", text:"The LORD forbid that I should stretch forth mine hand against the LORD's anointed: but, I pray thee, take thou now the spear that is at his bolster, and the cruse of water, and let us go." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 26:21", text:"Then said Saul, I have sinned: return, my son David: for I will no more do thee harm, because my soul was precious in thine eyes this day: behold, I have played the fool, and have erred exceedingly." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Wait for the Lord's way of deliverance; what He has promised, He can accomplish without our sin.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What two things did David take from Saul's side in the camp?", choices:["His crown and scepter","His sword and shield","His signet and robe","His spear and the cruse of water"], a:3, why:"David and Abishai carried off the spear stuck at Saul's bolster and the cruse of water." },
    { q:"Why did David stop Abishai from killing Saul?", choices:["Who can stretch forth his hand against the LORD's anointed, and be guiltless? The LORD would smite him","Abishai was too young to fight","The camp guards had awakened","David wanted Saul's spear instead"], a:0, why:"David said the LORD would smite Saul, or his day would come to die, or he would perish in battle — but David's hand would not be on him." },
    { q:"What kept the whole camp asleep while David and Abishai worked?", choices:["The men had drunk wine all night","A deep sleep from the LORD was fallen upon them","The guards were posted too far away to hear","David had drugged the watch fire"], a:1, why:"No man saw them or awoke, because a deep sleep from the LORD had fallen on the camp." }
  ] },
  { n:27, summary:"David said in his heart that he would one day perish by the hand of Saul, so the best course was to escape into the land of the Philistines. He passed over with his six hundred men to Achish king of Gath, along with his two wives, Ahinoam and Abigail. When Saul heard it, he sought no more for him. David asked for a town in the country rather than the royal city, and Achish gave him Ziklag, which thenceforth pertained unto the kings of Judah. David dwelt in the Philistine country a full year and four months, raiding the Geshurites, the Gezrites, and the Amalekites and leaving neither man nor woman alive, while carrying away sheep, oxen, asses, camels, and apparel. Asked whither he had made a road, David said against the south of Judah and the Jerahmeelites and Kenites — for he left no survivors to carry the news to Gath. Achish believed David had made himself odious to his own people and concluded he would be Achish's servant for ever.",
  context:"War-weary David defects to Achish of Gath and receives Ziklag, raiding desert peoples while reporting to the Philistine king.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 27:1", text:"And David said in his heart, I shall now perish one day by the hand of Saul: there is nothing better for me than that I should speedily escape into the land of the Philistines; and Saul shall despair of me, to seek me any more in any coast of Israel: so shall I escape out of his hand." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 27:6", text:"Then Achish gave him Ziklag that day: wherefore Ziklag pertaineth unto the kings of Judah unto this day." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 27:7", text:"And the time that David dwelt in the country of the Philistines was a full year and four months." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Reasoning from fear instead of faith can lead to compromises that complicate everything that follows.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Which Philistine king gave David the town of Ziklag?", choices:["Nahash","Agag","Achish","Goliath's brother"], a:2, why:"Achish, the son of Maoch king of Gath, gave David Ziklag that day." },
    { q:"How long did David dwell in the country of the Philistines?", choices:["Seven months","One year","Two years and six months","A full year and four months"], a:3, why:"The text states that David dwelt in the Philistine country a full year and four months." },
    { q:"Why did David leave neither man nor woman alive in his raids?", choices:["So no survivor could report to Gath what he was really doing","Because the law of Moses required it for every war","To collect double spoil from the fallen","Because Achish commanded it"], a:0, why:"David saved neither man nor woman alive to bring tidings to Gath, lest his true raids be discovered." }
  ] },
  { n:28, summary:"As the Philistines gathered for war, Samuel was dead, and Saul had banished those with familiar spirits and the wizards. The Philistines camped at Shunem and Israel at Gilboa, and when Saul saw the host his heart trembled. He inquired of the LORD, but was answered not, neither by dreams, nor by Urim, nor by prophets. Desperate, Saul sought a woman that hath a familiar spirit at En-dor. Disguised, by night, and under oath, he asked her to bring up Samuel. The woman cried out when she saw Samuel, and Saul confessed who he was; he said God had departed from him and answered him no more. Samuel's word was severe: the LORD had rent the kingdom out of his hand and given it to David, because he obeyed not the voice of the LORD nor executed his fierce wrath upon Amalek — and to morrow shalt thou and thy sons be with me. Saul fell strengthless to the earth; the woman fed him, and they went away that night.",
  context:"Israel and Philistia array at Gilboa and Shunem on the eve of battle; heaven silent to Saul, he seeks out a woman with a familiar spirit at En-dor.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 28:6", text:"And when Saul inquired of the LORD, the LORD answered him not, neither by dreams, nor by Urim, nor by prophets." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 28:16", text:"Then said Samuel, Wherefore then dost thou ask of me, seeing the LORD is departed from thee, and is become thine enemy?" },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 28:18", text:"Because thou obeyedst not the voice of the LORD, nor executedst his fierce wrath upon Amalek, therefore hath the LORD done this thing unto thee this day." }
  ],
  takeaway:"When we shut out the Lord, no substitute source of guidance can give peace; obedience keeps revelation open.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"By what three means did Saul try to inquire of the LORD without answer?", choices:["Sacrifice, fasting, and pilgrimage","Dreams, Urim, and prophets","Angels, visions, and lots","Priests, scribes, and elders"], a:1, why:"The LORD answered Saul not, neither by dreams, nor by Urim, nor by prophets." },
    { q:"Where did Saul's servants find the woman with a familiar spirit?", choices:["Shunem","Ramah","En-dor","Aphek"], a:2, why:"The servants told Saul there was a woman that hath a familiar spirit at En-dor." },
    { q:"What did Samuel's message to Saul include?", choices:["Instructions for winning the battle the next day","A promise that Israel would be spared","A call for Saul to repent and be restored","The kingdom was rent from him and given to David, and to morrow shalt thou and thy sons be with me"], a:3, why:"Samuel repeated the LORD's judgment over Amalek and foretold that Israel, Saul, and his sons would fall to the Philistines." }
  ] },
  { n:29, summary:"The Philistine armies gathered at Aphek, with Israel pitching by a fountain in Jezreel, and David marched in the rearward with Achish. The princes of the Philistines demanded, What do these Hebrews here? Achish defended David as blameless since the day he fell unto him, but the lords were wroth and ordered him sent back, lest in the battle he turn adversary and reconcile himself to his master with the heads of the Philistines — they remembered the song, Saul slew his thousands, and David his ten thousands. Achish broke the news gently: surely, as the LORD liveth, thou hast been upright... nevertheless the lords favour thee not; return, and go in peace. David protested his innocence, but Achish affirmed he was good in his sight as an angel of God while repeating that the princes would not have him in the battle. David and his men rose early and returned to the land of the Philistines, and the Philistines went up to Jezreel.",
  context:"The Philistine muster at Aphek for the Jezreel campaign forces the lords to decide whether David may march with them.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 29:4", text:"And the princes of the Philistines were wroth with him; and the princes of the Philistines said unto him, Make this fellow return, that he may go again to his place which thou hast appointed him, and let him not go down with us to battle" },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 29:6", text:"Then Achish called David, and said unto him, Surely, as the LORD liveth, thou hast been upright, and thy going out and thy coming in with me in the host is good in my sight" },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 29:9", text:"And Achish answered and said to David, I know that thou art good in my sight, as an angel of God: notwithstanding the princes of the Philistines have said, He shall not go up with us to the battle." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God can use even rejection to keep His servants out of compromises they would regret.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Where did the Philistines gather all their armies?", choices:["Aphek","Gath","Ekron","Michmash"], a:0, why:"The Philistines gathered all their armies to Aphek, while Israel pitched by a fountain in Jezreel." },
    { q:"Why did the princes of the Philistines refuse to let David go to battle?", choices:["They feared he would desert to Judah's enemies","Lest in the battle he turn adversary and reconcile himself to his master with their heads","Achish had already promised the command to another","David had broken his oath to Achish"], a:1, why:"The lords warned that David might turn in the battle and reconcile himself to Saul with the heads of the Philistine soldiers." },
    { q:"How did Achish describe David even while sending him home?", choices:["As a traitor in disguise","As too weak to fight","Good in his sight as an angel of God, though the lords favoured him not","As an ungrateful servant"], a:2, why:"Achish told David he had found no fault in him and called him good in his sight as an angel of God." }
  ] },
  { n:30, summary:"Ziklag burned: returning on the third day, David and his men found it destroyed by Amalekites, their families carried off, none slain. The people wept until they had no more power to weep; David's two wives were gone, and men spoke of stoning him. But David encouraged himself in the LORD his God and, through Abiathar's ephod, inquired whether to pursue. The LORD answered, Pursue: for thou shalt surely overtake them, and without fail recover all. Four hundred crossed the brook Besor; two hundred stayed, too faint. An abandoned Egyptian servant of an Amalekite, revived with food and water, led them to the raiders, feasting on the spoil. David smote them from twilight to the next evening, and only four hundred camel riders escaped; David recovered all and rescued his two wives. When wicked men proposed denying spoil to those who stayed behind, David ruled: as his part is that goeth down to the battle, so shall his part be that tarrieth by the stuff. He sent presents of the spoil to elders of Judah, even to Hebron.",
  context:"Returning from the Philistine muster, David finds Ziklag burned by Amalekites and pursues them from the brook Besor to their desert camp.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 30:6", text:"And David was greatly distressed; for the people spake of stoning him, because the soul of all the people was grieved, every man for his sons and for his daughters: but David encouraged himself in the LORD his God." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 30:8", text:"And David inquired at the LORD, saying, Shall I pursue after this troop? shall I overtake them? And he answered him, Pursue: for thou shalt surely overtake them, and without fail recover all." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 30:24", text:"For who will hearken unto you in this matter? but as his part is that goeth down to the battle, so shall his part be that tarrieth by the stuff: they shall part alike." }
  ],
  takeaway:"When everything falls apart, encourage yourself in the Lord, seek His direction, and then act.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How many men crossed the brook Besor with David, and how many stayed behind?", choices:["Four hundred went; two hundred stayed","Three hundred went; three hundred stayed","All six hundred went","Five hundred went; one hundred stayed"], a:0, why:"Four hundred went with David while two hundred stayed behind, too faint to cross the brook Besor." },
    { q:"How did David strengthen himself when the men spoke of stoning him?", choices:["By calling for his spear and guards","He encouraged himself in the LORD his God and inquired of Him through the ephod","By fleeing to Achish at once","By making a treaty with the Amalekites"], a:1, why:"David was greatly distressed but encouraged himself in the LORD his God, then asked the LORD through Abiathar's ephod whether to pursue." },
    { q:"What did David's statute after the victory say about the spoil?", choices:["Only the fighting men could share it","The king received a tenth of everything","It was given entirely to the elders of Judah","As his part is that goeth down to the battle, so shall his part be that tarrieth by the stuff"], a:3, why:"David ruled that those who stayed by the stuff would share alike with those who fought, and it became a statute in Israel." }
  ] },
  { n:31, summary:"The Philistines fought against Israel, and the men of Israel fled and fell slain in mount Gilboa. Pursuing hard after Saul and his sons, the Philistines slew Jonathan, Abinadab, and Malchi-shua; the battle went sore against Saul, and archers wounded him. Lest the uncircumcised abuse him, Saul asked his armourbearer to thrust him through; the man feared, and Saul took a sword and fell upon it. His armourbearer died likewise, and Saul, his three sons, his armourbearer, and all his men died that same day. When Israelites beyond the valley saw the army fled and Saul dead, they forsook their cities, and the Philistines dwelt in them. Next day the Philistines found the bodies, cut off Saul's head, stripped his armour, sent the news into the land of their idols, fastened his armour in the house of Ashtaroth, and pinned his body to the wall of Beth-shan. The valiant men of Jabesh-gilead traveled all night, took down the bodies, burned them at Jabesh, buried the bones under a tree, and fasted seven days.",
  context:"The war foretold in chapter 28 arrives: Israel falls on mount Gilboa, and Saul's tragedy ends near Beth-shan with loyal mourning at Jabesh-gilead.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"1 Samuel 31:4", text:"Then said Saul unto his armourbearer, Draw thy sword, and thrust me through therewith; lest these uncircumcised come and thrust me through, and abuse me. But his armourbearer would not; for he was sore afraid. Therefore Saul took a sword, and fell upon it." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 31:6", text:"So Saul died, and his three sons, and his armourbearer, and all his men, that same day together." },
    { ref:"1 Samuel 31:12", text:"All the valiant men arose, and went all night, and took the body of Saul and the bodies of his sons from the wall of Beth-shan, and came to Jabesh, and burnt them there." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Life apart from God ends in tragedy, yet loyalty and honor can still be shown to the fallen.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Which three sons of Saul died with him on mount Gilboa?", choices:["Joel, Abiah, and Abner","Jonathan, Ishui, and Merab","Jonathan, Abinadab, and Malchi-shua","Ishui, Abinadab, and Abner"], a:2, why:"The Philistines slew Jonathan, Abinadab, and Malchi-shua, Saul's sons, as they pursued hard after him." },
    { q:"Which town's valiant men retrieved Saul's body from the wall of Beth-shan?", choices:["Gibeah","Hebron","Ziklag","Jabesh-gilead"], a:3, why:"The valiant men of Jabesh-gilead traveled all night, took down the bodies, and burned and buried them at Jabesh." },
    { q:"Why did Saul fall on his own sword?", choices:["His armourbearer refused to thrust him through, and he would not let the uncircumcised abuse him","Samuel commanded it as a sacrifice","His sword was poisoned by the Philistines","He wanted to be buried in Gibeah"], a:0, why:"When his armourbearer feared to kill him, Saul took a sword and fell upon it rather than be abused by the Philistines." }
  ] }
);
